WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:16.621
Unit twenty-one: Disability Rights. Today you will refer to groups respectfully with the + adjective and person-first language.

4
00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:19.411
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.704
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.609
Disability.

7
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:30.776
Disability means a condition that makes some activities more difficult.

8
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:34.347
A disability should never limit a person's rights.

9
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.660
Your turn. Say: Disability.

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.710
Disability.

11
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.347
Rights.

12
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.230
Rights means freedoms and protections that every person is entitled to.

13
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:51.873
The convention sets out the rights of every person.

14
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:54.753
Your turn. Say: Rights.

15
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.601
Rights.

16
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.757
Accessibility.

17
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.851
Accessibility means the quality of being easy to reach, enter or use.

18
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.272
Accessibility includes ramps, lifts and clear signs.

19
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:14.244
Your turn. Say: Accessibility.

20
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.426
Accessibility.

21
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:20.277
Inclusion.

22
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:24.247
Inclusion means making sure everyone can take part.

23
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.767
Inclusion in schools benefits all students.

24
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:30.989
Your turn. Say: Inclusion.

25
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:34.869
Inclusion.

26
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:37.001
Discrimination.

27
00:01:38.017 --> 00:01:41.653
Discrimination means unfair treatment because of who a person is.

28
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.900
Discrimination in hiring is against the law.

29
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:48.426
Your turn. Say: Discrimination.

30
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.570
Discrimination.

31
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:54.051
Law.

32
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.454
Law means a rule made by a government that everyone must follow.

33
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.885
The law requires new buildings to be accessible.

34
00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:04.764
Your turn. Say: Law.

35
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.497
Law.

36
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.448
Advocacy.

37
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.413
Advocacy means public support for a cause or group.

38
00:02:15.017 --> 00:02:17.504
Advocacy brought about the new transport rules.

39
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:20.943
Your turn. Say: Advocacy.

40
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.894
Advocacy.

41
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.220
Equal opportunity.

42
00:02:28.217 --> 00:02:31.911
Equal opportunity means the same chance for everyone to succeed.

43
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:35.642
Equal opportunity means a fair chance at every job.

44
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.680
Your turn. Say: Equal opportunity.

45
00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:44.161
Equal opportunity.

46
00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:48.803
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:57.608
Visal, a report says 'Public transport should serve the elderly and people with disabilities.' Why 'the elderly' but 'people with disabilities'?

48
00:02:58.717 --> 00:03:13.281
Some adjectives work as plural nouns with 'the': the elderly, the young, the rich, with a plural verb. Many people prefer person-first language, such as 'people with disabilities', which names the person first.

49
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:16.441
Does language matter for rights?

50
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:29.795
Yes. Respectful terms support inclusion and reduce discrimination. We avoid 'the disabled' as a label in much modern guidance, and we never say 'a disabled' as a noun.

51
00:03:30.883 --> 00:03:32.871
Which structure should I use in law?

52
00:03:33.983 --> 00:03:45.215
Use 'people who have a disability' or 'persons with disabilities', as in many laws. 'The elderly are protected' is acceptable, but 'an elderly' is wrong.

53
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:48.088
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.539
Grammar focus: The plus adjective for groups; person-first language.

55
00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:55.083
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:55.783 --> 00:03:59.478
Public transport should serve the elderly and people with disabilities.

57
00:04:00.383 --> 00:04:03.915
The law protects people with a disability from discrimination.

58
00:04:04.817 --> 00:04:07.989
Accessibility helps the young and the old equally.

59
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:18.972
the + adjective = a group, with a plural verb (the elderly are). Never use it for one person (not an elderly). Prefer person-first phrases: people with disabilities.

60
00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:22.973
Be careful! Don't say: An elderly needs a ramp.

61
00:04:24.933 --> 00:04:27.467
Say: The elderly need a ramp.

62
00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.777
'The elderly' is plural: use the + adjective.

63
00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:34.242
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:36.557
Access for Everyone.

65
00:04:36.857 --> 00:04:42.072
Disability rights began as a demand for equal opportunity, not for special treatment.

66
00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:48.828
The law now protects people with disabilities from discrimination in education, work and public life.

67
00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:55.978
Accessibility, such as ramps, clear signs and digital tools, helps the elderly and the young as well.

68
00:04:56.967 --> 00:05:02.101
Inclusion means designing places so that no one needs to ask for permission to enter.

69
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.141
Advocacy groups remind governments that the rights of the vulnerable are everyone's responsibility.

70
00:05:09.233 --> 00:05:13.131
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.983 --> 00:05:16.285
The elderly blank protected by this law.

72
00:05:16.585 --> 00:05:19.971
A, is. B, are. C, has.

73
00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:25.127
The answer is B: are.

74
00:05:26.283 --> 00:05:28.817
Which phrase uses person-first language?

75
00:05:29.117 --> 00:05:33.571
A, the disabled. B, a disabled. C, people with disabilities.

76
00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:40.607
The answer is C: people with disabilities.

77
00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.541
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:43.841 --> 00:05:49.398
A, An elderly needs a ramp.. B, The elderly need a ramp.. C, The elderlies need a ramp..

79
00:05:52.933 --> 00:05:55.722
The answer is B: The elderly need a ramp..

80
00:05:56.883 --> 00:06:00.183
Designing places so that everyone can use them is blank.

81
00:06:00.483 --> 00:06:04.450
A, inclusion. B, advocacy. C, discrimination.

82
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.243
The answer is A: inclusion.

83
00:06:11.383 --> 00:06:15.809
According to the reading, disability rights began as a demand for equal blank.

84
00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:19.798
A, opportunity. B, salary. C, speed.

85
00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:25.495
The answer is A: opportunity.

86
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.689
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.409
How accessible are public places in your city?

88
00:06:42.217 --> 00:06:43.636
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.936 --> 00:07:15.374
In many cities, public places are only partly accessible. New buildings often include ramps and lifts, but older streets may have high kerbs, uneven pavements and no tactile paving. Public transport may lack space for wheelchairs, and signs are rarely available in simple or braille formats. Better design and strong accessibility laws would help everyone move around safely and independently.

90
00:07:15.674 --> 00:07:17.268
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.183 --> 00:07:24.446
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and the plus adjective for groups; person-first language.

92
00:07:24.746 --> 00:07:30.150
Today's value is respect for differences. Everyone is different, and everyone is special.

93
00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.161
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:33.461 --> 00:07:36.090
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

